begin
  if not exists (select 1 from public.tests where id <> 'fe816150-8c12-4b3a-b04a-000000000002') then
    insert into public.tests (id, category_id, title, duration, published, description, section, difficulty, instructions, passage_title, passage_text, passage_image, access_rank, kind, version, sat_module_source, sat_session_form)
      select saved.id, category.id, saved.title, saved.duration, saved.published, saved.description, saved.section, saved.difficulty, saved.instructions, saved.passage_title, saved.passage_text, saved.passage_image, saved.access_rank, saved.kind, saved.version, saved.sat_module_source, saved.sat_session_form
      from jsonb_populate_recordset(null::public.tests, $catalog_json$[{"id":"2b07157d-ac83-5713-9ee9-ea7e0329dc2d","title":"SAT Math · Module 2","duration":35,"published":true,"description":"Original TAFE practice questions","section":"Math","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"","passage_title":"","passage_text":"","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"practice","version":2,"sat_module_source":true,"sat_session_form":false},{"id":"70767467-ea94-5677-9212-77a335806796","title":"IELTS Academic Reading · Practice Set 1","duration":60,"published":true,"description":"Original TAFE practice questions","section":"Academic Reading","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"","passage_title":"","passage_text":"A. Cooling a city block\n\nIn 2019, planners in the fictional city of Bellhaven began a three-year study of summer heat. They selected twelve streets with similar traffic levels but different amounts of tree cover. Sensors at pedestrian height recorded the air temperature every fifteen minutes. A separate instrument measured the temperature of road surfaces, since a cool surface does not necessarily mean that the surrounding air has cooled by the same amount.\n\nOn afternoons with clear skies, streets with mature trees were generally cooler than treeless streets. The largest difference appeared shortly after two in the afternoon. At night, however, the pattern was less consistent. Buildings sometimes prevented heat from escaping, and trees did not erase differences caused by building height. The research team warned that its measurements did not establish whether the same pattern would hold in coastal cities.\n\nBellhaven then tested a second intervention. Workers painted part of a school car park with a reflective coating. The surface cooled, but some pedestrians found the reflected sunlight uncomfortable. The council decided to monitor glare as well as temperature before using the coating near busy crossings. It also emphasized that trees and reflective surfaces could serve different purposes. Trees offered shade at walking height, while a coated roof could reduce the amount of heat entering a building.\n\nThe council’s final report recommended protecting existing mature trees and planting a variety of species. It did not promise immediate results from planting. Young trees need time to grow, and new plantings require regular watering during their first summers.\n\nB. Restoring a seed collection\n\nA university archive kept seeds collected during agricultural surveys in the early twentieth century. For decades, the collection was used mostly as a historical record. When a new laboratory opened, a team examined whether any seeds could still germinate. Labels were digitized before the packets were opened so that handwritten notes would remain available even if the paper was damaged during handling.\n\nThe team did not test every packet. It selected samples from groups with clear collection dates and enough seeds to retain some untouched material. For each selected group, one set was placed on moist paper at a constant temperature. Another set received a brief cool treatment first. Some species performed better after cooling, but others showed little difference. The team therefore resisted presenting one treatment as universally effective.\n\nA striking result came from a sample of barley. Although most of its seeds failed, a small number produced seedlings. The scientists moved them to an isolated greenhouse to prevent accidental crossing with modern varieties. They planned to compare growth habits with recent barley varieties, but the first report presented only germination results.\n\nThe project’s managers also changed how new accessions were stored. Each was divided between two locations so that a single equipment failure would be less likely to destroy the entire sample. A guide explained how researchers could request material and why requests for rare samples required additional review. The archive continued to lend photographs of the original labels without that review.\n\nC. Measuring sound in a library\n\nA library introduced a trial in which one floor was designated for quiet work and another for group discussion. Before the trial, librarians counted occupied seats at fixed times and asked visitors to complete a short anonymous questionnaire. The survey asked whether people could find an appropriate place to work. It did not collect their names or ask what they were studying.\n\nAfter the change, demand for quiet seats was highest in the two weeks before final examinations. Groups tended to arrive later in the day. When some groups moved chairs into corridors, the library installed extra tables on the discussion floor. It did not increase the size of the quiet floor during the trial. Staff also noticed that sound from a stairwell travelled between floors despite the separation.\n\nTo investigate the stairwell problem, the library placed sound meters near two doors. The meters recorded sound levels, not speech. One door was fitted with a soft closer, while the other door was left unchanged for comparison. The maximum sound level near the modified door fell during the observation period. The library nevertheless noted that door usage could have differed and that the experiment did not show how much individual visitors were disturbed.\n\nThe final report recommended keeping both zones. It also recommended a trial of desk lamps, as some visitors said that the available lighting limited their choice of seats. The report did not claim that the quiet zone improved grades. It proposed repeating the survey in another term to see whether preferences remained stable outside the examination period.","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"practice","version":1,"sat_module_source":false,"sat_session_form":false},{"id":"9c4d7708-f642-5cca-9458-57c66aa2f1b7","title":"SAT Reading and Writing · Module 2","duration":32,"published":true,"description":"Original TAFE practice questions","section":"Reading and Writing","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"","passage_title":"","passage_text":"","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"practice","version":2,"sat_module_source":true,"sat_session_form":false},{"id":"e4b994df-8f8f-582a-bba2-e53c002d825a","title":"SAT Reading and Writing · Module 1","duration":32,"published":true,"description":"Original TAFE practice questions","section":"Reading and Writing","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"","passage_title":"","passage_text":"","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"practice","version":2,"sat_module_source":true,"sat_session_form":false},{"id":"e74e5cb0-f8b8-5c37-bf33-7026c247b906","title":"SAT Math · Module 1","duration":35,"published":true,"description":"Original TAFE practice questions","section":"Math","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"","passage_title":"","passage_text":"","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"practice","version":2,"sat_module_source":true,"sat_session_form":false},{"id":"fe816150-8c12-4b3a-b04a-000000000001","title":"SAT · Full Practice","duration":134,"published":true,"description":"98 questions across four timed modules. One result for the entire test.","section":"Reading and Writing + Math","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"Complete both Reading and Writing modules, then both Math modules. Earlier modules lock when you move on.","passage_title":"","passage_text":"","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"practice","version":1,"sat_module_source":false,"sat_session_form":true},{"id":"fe816150-8c12-4b3a-b04a-000000000011","title":"CEFR · Multilevel Reading","duration":20,"published":true,"description":"Two original reading texts with twelve practice questions.","section":"Reading","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"Read both texts and choose one answer for each question.","passage_title":"Reading passages A and B","passage_text":"A. A library of things\n\nWhen the Riverside Library opened a tool-lending service, its staff expected the busiest users to be experienced home repairers. The first month produced a surprise: many visitors had never used a drill or repaired a bicycle. To help people get started, volunteers began offering short demonstrations on Saturday mornings. Borrowers could reserve equipment for three days, and the demonstrations were useful even for people who did not intend to borrow anything that week.\n\nThe service started with tools donated by local households, but not everything offered could be used. Several items were too heavy for the shelves, and some lacked safety instructions. The library kept only equipment it could check and maintain. It also introduced a return checklist so users could report missing parts. Staff judged the first year by how often the equipment was returned ready for its next borrower, as well as by the number of loans.\n\nB. The cost of a cooler street\n\nIn the fictional city of Norcott, researchers compared two streets before and after one received new shade structures. The streets had similar traffic levels and building heights, but only the shaded street also gained several benches. On warm afternoons, people spent longer on the improved street. Evening observations, however, showed little difference between the two locations.\n\nThe council initially credited the shade for the increase. The researchers were more cautious: they had measured where people stayed, but had not separated the effect of shade from the opportunity to sit down. They proposed a follow-up study in which shade and seating would be installed independently at different sites. They also recommended collecting data during very hot weather, because the initial observations had taken place in a relatively mild summer.\n\nThe report did not reject the project. Rather, it argued that an intervention can be worthwhile even when the reason it succeeds is still uncertain. If the council understood which changes mattered most, it could invest in a better combination of improvements on streets with less space.","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"practice","version":1,"sat_module_source":false,"sat_session_form":false},{"id":"fe816150-8c12-4b3a-b04a-000000000031","title":"Digital SAT · Adaptive Simulation","duration":134,"published":true,"description":"OpenSAT question bank · TAFE adaptive simulation","section":"Reading and Writing + Math","difficulty":"intermediate","instructions":"Complete all four modules in order. Answer review unlocks after submission.","passage_title":"","passage_text":"","passage_image":null,"access_rank":0,"kind":"mock","version":1,"sat_module_source":false,"sat_session_form":true}]$catalog_json$::jsonb) saved
      join (values
      ('2b07157d-ac83-5713-9ee9-ea7e0329dc2d'::uuid, 'sat'),
      ('70767467-ea94-5677-9212-77a335806796'::uuid, 'ielts'),
      ('9c4d7708-f642-5cca-9458-57c66aa2f1b7'::uuid, 'sat'),
      ('e4b994df-8f8f-582a-bba2-e53c002d825a'::uuid, 'sat'),
      ('e74e5cb0-f8b8-5c37-bf33-7026c247b906'::uuid, 'sat'),
      ('fe816150-8c12-4b3a-b04a-000000000001'::uuid, 'sat'),
      ('fe816150-8c12-4b3a-b04a-000000000011'::uuid, 'cefr'),
      ('fe816150-8c12-4b3a-b04a-000000000031'::uuid, 'sat')
